    id: "9245526c32e41c38bde3",
    cat: "geopolitica",
    categories: ["geopolitica"],
    title: "Zimbabwe issues arrest warrant for Robert Mugabe's son over missed court date",
    summary: "The son of Zimbabwe's late former President Robert Mugabe has faced multiple legal troubles in the recent past.",
    preview: "A Zimbabwean court has issued an arrest warrant for Bellarmine Chatunga Mugabe, the youngest son of the country's late former president Robert Mugabe, after he failed to appear in court. Mugabe was arrested earlier this month alongside four others over assault allegations on a police officer during an investigation into the suspected smuggling of luxury vehicles. The group subsequently faced charges of robbery and malicious damage to property. They have yet to enter pleas. They were granted bail on 6 November and were due back in court on Tuesday.",
    body: "A Zimbabwean court has issued an arrest warrant for Bellarmine Chatunga Mugabe, the youngest son of the country's late former president Robert Mugabe, after he failed to appear in court.\n\nMugabe was arrested earlier this month alongside four others over assault allegations on a police officer during an investigation into the suspected smuggling of luxury vehicles.\n\nThe group subsequently faced charges of robbery and malicious damage to property. They have yet to enter pleas.\n\nThey were granted bail on 6 November and were due back in court on Tuesday. However, Mugabe failed to attend. His co-accused, an aide and three security guards appeared and were remanded until 10 December.\n\nProsecutors allege that Mugabe and the others assaulted an investigating officer using their hands and broomsticks.\n\nThe officer's phone was allegedly taken and smashed on the ground, while one of the accused is said to have seized the officer's loaded pistol.\n\nBellarmine Chatunga is the youngest son of Robert Mugabe and his second wife, Grace Mugabe.\n\nRobert Mugabe ruled Zimbabwe for 37 years before being forced from power in 2017, aged 93, following a military intervention. He died in 2019.\n\nHis son has attracted public attention in recent years over a series of legal troubles and his high-profile lifestyle.\n\nLast month, he admitted to drug-related charges involving the possession of cannabis.\n\nIn April, he was deported from neighbouring South Africa after pleading guilty to pointing a firearm and being in the country illegally. He had been arrested following the shooting of a 23-year-old man at his home.\n\nIn 2023, he was arrested in Harare, Zimbabwe's capital, over allegations that he damaged property and spat at a police officer during a party.\n\nHe was later freed after reaching an out-of-court settlement with the complainant, who was a friend.\n\nYou may also be interested in:\n\n- Robert Mugabe: From liberator to tyrant\n\n- Published6 September 2019\n\nGo to BBCAfrica.com, external for more news from the African continent.\n\nFollow us on Twitter @BBCAfrica, external, on Facebook at BBC Africa, external or on Instagram at bbcafrica, external",
