    cat: "geopolitica",
    categories: ["geopolitica"],
    title: "Kilmar Ábrego García leaves US for Spain, ending Trump deportation battle",
    summary: "Ábrego García's deportation to El Salvador and return to the US became a flashpoint in the debate over President Donald Trump's immigration agenda.",
    preview: "Kilmar Ãbrego GarcÃa leaves US for Spain, ending Trump deportation battle Kilmar Ãbrego GarcÃa, whose deportation last year to El Salvador sparked a bitter deportation battle, has left the US for Spain. He took a commercial flight to the country on Tuesday. After the government admitted it had wrongfully sent Ãbrego GarcÃa to his home country, he was returned to the US to face human trafficking charges, which were later dropped. The administration then continued its attempts to send him to other countries, including Uganda and Ghana.",
    body: "Kilmar Ãbrego GarcÃa leaves US for Spain, ending Trump deportation battle\n\nKilmar Ãbrego GarcÃa, whose deportation last year to El Salvador sparked a bitter deportation battle, has left the US for Spain.\n\nHe took a commercial flight to the country on Tuesday.\n\nAfter the government admitted it had wrongfully sent Ãbrego GarcÃa to his home country, he was returned to the US to face human trafficking charges, which were later dropped. The administration then continued its attempts to send him to other countries, including Uganda and Ghana.\n\nHis deportation and return became a flashpoint in the debate over President Donald Trump's immigration agenda.\n\nÃbrego GarcÃa, who is married to a US citizen and lived in Maryland, came to the US illegally from El Salvador as a teenager.\n\nSenator Chris Van Hollen, a Democrat from Maryland, visited Ãbrego GarcÃa while he was in the notorious Cecot prison in El Salvador in 2025 and advocated for his release.\n\n\"A year and a half ago, the Trump Administration wanted to leave Kilmar Ãbrego GarcÃa to rot in a Salvadoran gulag,\" Van Hollen said in a statement on Tuesday. \"Today, Kilmar is travelling to a country of his choice, a free man - unharmed and with dignity.\"\n\nIn a statement, Ãbrego GarcÃa's lawyer said his departure was \"temporary\" as he had \"many options to return to the United States\".\n\n\"We don't expect that to happen under this administration, which we know will never give him a fair shake, but we expect that the next administration will prioritize reunifying this family and bringing him back to Maryland,\" the statement said.\n\n\"Where he goes from here, where he chooses to settle for the next two years while he waits out the end of this administration, will be his choice, not the administration's choice.\"\n\nThe BBC has contacted the US Department of Homeland Security for comment.\n\nÃbrego GarcÃa was arrested in 2019 alongside three other men and detained by federal immigration authorities. A judge granted him protection from deportation to El Salvador at the time, citing the risk of persecution by a gang there.\n\nIn March 2025, the Trump administration deported him to El Salvador, where he was held in Cecot, a prison known for its harsh conditions. The US Supreme Court then ordered the government to facilitate his return.\n\nThe administration admitted that he was deported in error and brought him back to the US where he was set to face human trafficking charges. A federal judge dismissed the case, saying Ãbrego GarcÃa had been charged for political reasons.\n\nFor months, he was locked in a battle over deportation with the government.\n\nÃbrego GarcÃa had repeatedly said he would be willing to be deported to Costa Rica, and the Costa Rican government had agreed to accept him as a refugee and provide him with legal status.\n\nAfter reports emerged that the administration was considering sending him to Uganda, a judge barred the government from deporting Ãbrego GarcÃa to a third country.\n\nAt one point the Trump administration requested that the judge lift the order so that Ãbrego GarcÃa could be deported to Liberia, CBS reported.\n\n- Published22 May\n\n- Published30 April 2025\n\n- Published9 April 2025",
