    id: "08b7c34149b316b733bf",
    cat: "geopolitica",
    categories: ["geopolitica"],
    title: "The U.S.-China-Russia summit was no new Yalta",
    summary: "The Shenzhen summit was more a public relations exercise than a meaningful meeting of three strategically aligned powers.",
    preview: "Last week, the leaders of the United States, China and Russia held their first-ever trilateral summit in Shenzhen, China. Reactions in Japan were sharply divided. Prime Minister Sanae Takaichi declared that “Japan’s interests will not be compromised” and that “we wish to maintain dialogue with China.” Chief Cabinet Secretary Minoru Kihara struck a similarly measured tone, saying Japan would “continue to monitor the situation” and “maintain close communication with allies and like-minded nations to work toward maintaining and strengthening a free and…",
    body: "Last week, the leaders of the United States, China and Russia held their first-ever trilateral summit in Shenzhen, China.\n\nReactions in Japan were sharply divided. Prime Minister Sanae Takaichi declared that “Japan’s interests will not be compromised” and that “we wish to maintain dialogue with China.” Chief Cabinet Secretary Minoru Kihara struck a similarly measured tone, saying Japan would “continue to monitor the situation” and “maintain close communication with allies and like-minded nations to work toward maintaining and strengthening a free and open international order.”\n\nBy contrast, the Japanese media reacted with sharp criticism and, in some cases, alarm. One commentator, under the headline “U.S.-China-Russia Summit Exposes Prime Minister Takaichi’s ‘Diplomatic Defeat’: How Will Japan Confront the ‘New Yalta System’ in Which the Three Great Powers Control the World Order?” argued that “an overhaul of Japanese diplomacy is required.” Others suggested that China’s “blatant snub” of Takaichi, including scaled-back welcome ceremonies, might actually strengthen her conservative support base.",
