    id: "5003e9f46ed5cbf34ada",
    cat: "conflitti",
    categories: ["conflitti"],
    title: "Burnham’s devolution plans risk ‘significant disparities’ for people in England, MPs say – UK politics live",
    summary: "Public accounts committee warns of ‘disparities in opportunities and outcomes’ depending on the type of strategic authority where people live Millions of British households will face annual energy bills of more than £2,000 a year for the first time since Russia’s invasion of Ukraine, as the cap on energy bills climbs for a third straight quarter, Jillian Ambrose reports. Commenting on the increase for the Conservatives, Andrew Bowie , the shadow energy secretary, said: It is an outrage that, as bills soar, Labour are not only piling green levies and taxes on to bills but signing us up to an EU scheme that risks pushing them even higher. Labour are utterly out of touch and could fix this tomorrow if they had the backbone. Right now, millions of families feel completely powerless in the face of reckless wars driving up energy prices, and they are being left to fend for themselves while bills spiral out of control. The meagre and poorly targeted support the government has promised will be wiped out by this rise, and many of those who most desperately need help won’t get a penny. The government needs to wake up to the scale of this crisis. Labour and the Tories net zero obsession is going to bankrupt Britain. We’re paying the price for Trump’s war in Iran & our failure to get off the fossil fuel rollercoaster coaster The UK supported that war, with US bombers launching from here It doesn’t have to be like this. We could transform our economy to be entirely based on clean, affordable energy. And we could ban the US from using our bases for good, and end our tacit support for their wars of aggression. Continue reading...",
    preview: "Public accounts committee warns of ‘disparities in opportunities and outcomes’ depending on the type of strategic authority where people live Millions of British households will face annual energy bills of more than £2,000 a year for the first time since Russia’s invasion of Ukraine, as the cap on energy bills climbs for a third straight quarter, Jillian Ambrose reports.",
    body: "Asked about the row on BBC Breakfast, Phillipson said:\n\nWe can’t control everything that people say or do, but I am not happy with that video. I’m glad it’s been taken down, but it was totally unacceptable.\n\nWhat I do think is right, however, is that as well as politicians like me coming on your programme to reach all of your viewers, that we seek to reach a wide audience through all of those people that will consume their media, their news on social media platforms, on podcasts.\n\nWe want to reach people where they are. That’s what the prime minister is seeking to do.\n\nBurnham urged to step in to halt energy bill hikes in January, as Ofgem says price cap to rise 26%\n\nMillions of British households will face annual energy bills of more than £2,000 a year for the first time since Russia’s invasion of Ukraine, as the cap on energy bills climbs for a third straight quarter, Jillian Ambrose reports.\n\nCommenting on the increase for the Conservatives, Andrew Bowie, the shadow energy secretary, said:\n\nIt is an outrage that, as bills soar, Labour are not only piling green levies and taxes on to bills but signing us up to an EU scheme that risks pushing them even higher.\n\nLabour are utterly out of touch and could fix this tomorrow if they had the backbone.\n\nAnd Ed Davey, the Lib Dem leader, said:\n\nRight now, millions of families feel completely powerless in the face of reckless wars driving up energy prices, and they are being left to fend for themselves while bills spiral out of control.\n\nThe meagre and poorly targeted support the government has promised will be wiped out by this rise, and many of those who most desperately need help won’t get a penny. The government needs to wake up to the scale of this crisis.\n\nThe prime minister must step in and stop such a massive hike going ahead in January. The Treasury and the energy network companies are both raking in huge windfalls because of this crisis - that cash should be used to keep bills down for struggling households, not left sitting in Treasury coffers and shareholders’ pockets.\n\nBurnham’s devolution plans risk ‘significant disparities’ for people in England, MPs say\n\nGood morning. Kemi Badenoch will be choosing what to raise at PMQs this morning and she’s spoilt for choice.\n\nIt would be very surprising if she did not raise the most important story of the morning – the news that gas and electricity prices will rise by 26% from January under Ofgem’s latest price cap.\n\nAnd, as someone who is herself over-focused on social media, it is likely that Badenoch will also want to raise the row about TikTok influencers in Downing Street. It is a story about preposterous media figures with no respect for the political establishment; but that’s enough about tabloid editors for now.\n\nOne thing unlikely to come up is the public accounts committee report out today on devolving power in England. So let’s give it some coverage anyway. Burnham has many political ambitions, but the one idea that seems to matter to him more than any other, and that marks him out from his predecessors, is his desire to devolve power out of Westminster, to let mayors and other local leaders have more clout and – via this devolution – to promote growth “in every postcode”.\n\nBut, given the way the policy is being implemented, this may not happen, says the committee, which has a Labour majority. It says:\n\nThere is a risk of significant disparities in opportunities and outcomes for people living in different parts of England, depending on what type of strategic authority they have.\n\nThe government has consciously adopted a differentiated approach to devolution. Areas that choose an elected mayor to lead their strategic authority (‘mayoral strategic authorities’) can access a broader range of devolved powers and funding flexibilities to improve local outcomes than those without a mayor.\n\nHowever, even among areas with a mayor, powers and arrangements differ. Only seven of England’s 18 mayoral strategic authorities receive an integrated settlement – a single funding pot for each area, collectively worth around £15.9bn up to 2029–30, that provides greater funding flexibilities and multi-year funding certainty.\n\nLondon also differs from other mayoral areas. Its constituent boroughs have no formal role in the Greater London Authority’s decisions or vote on its budget. This risks decisions being taken without formal input from the boroughs that are responsible for delivering local services, and may make lines of accountability less clear for local people.\n\nFiscal devolution could further deepen differences between areas. The government’s October 2026 Fiscal Devolution Roadmap confirms that only mayoral strategic authorities will be able to retain a share of local income tax. It proposes ways to limit disparities between different mayoral strategic authorities, but important details are still to be determined, including through the next spending review, and it therefore remains uncertain how effective these arrangements will be.\n\n3pm: Nigel Farage and Arron Banks launch their Keep Britain Out campaign opposing the idea of the UK rejoining the EU.\n\nI’m afraid we are no longer able to have comments open below the line every day on this blog. If you want to contact me, please email politics.live@theguardian.com. You can use this email to flag up corrections, comments for publication, questions or complaints.",
    content_status: "full",
    source: "The Guardian",
    url: "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2026/nov/25/andy-burnham-kemi-badenoch-pmqs-energy-bills-tiktok-welfare-benefits-timms-reform-brexit-devolution-latest-news-updates",
